## 5. Dependencies and layout

Runtime packages: shiny, bslib, imager, ggplot2. imager 1.0.8 is current on CRAN
(published 2025-12-23). dplyr and Momocs are removed. Silhouette width,
Procrustes alignment, Fourier analysis, and the RV coefficient are implemented
in base R as specified below.

Test-only packages: testthat; cluster (a recommended package shipped with R,
used to check the silhouette implementation). Fixture generation only: Momocs
1.4.1 (CRAN archive) and geomorph, used by `tests/fixtures/make_reference.R` to
write committed reference values. Tests read the committed values and do not
need Momocs. Deployment: rsconnect.

```
bifaceAnalysis/            deployed app directory
  app.R                    UI and server wiring only
  R/image.R                thresholding, mask selection, contour extraction
  R/outline.R              orientation, landmarks, resampling, measurements
  R/efa.R                  elliptical Fourier forward, inverse, power
  R/shape.R                Procrustes alignment, PCA, clustering, regions
  R/store.R                specimen records, CSV schema, image loading
  R/plots.R                one plotting function per figure (screen and PDF)
  R/interpret.R            interpretation text built from computed values
  R/report.R               PDF report
  www/                     demo images
tests/testthat/            unit and server tests (outside the deployed directory)
tests/fixtures/            reference values, reference masks, generating script
docs/specs/, docs/audits/
```

Tests and the fixture script sit outside `bifaceAnalysis/` so that rsconnect's
dependency scan does not record testthat, cluster, or Momocs for deployment.

## 6. Processing pipeline

All functions in this section are pure functions of their inputs, with no Shiny
dependency, so each can be unit tested.

### 6.1 Image to binary mask

Coordinates. "Original pixels" are pixels of the file. "Working pixels" are
pixels of the image after the resize in step 1. The resize factor f (working
divided by original, at most 1) is kept for each image. Every outline and
measurement is in working pixels of its own image.

1. Load JPEG or PNG. Drop any alpha channel. If the longer side exceeds 1200
   pixels, resize by linear interpolation so the longer side is 1200.
2. Optional crop from a drag rectangle. Default is the whole image.
3. Convert to grayscale. Compute a global threshold by Otsu's method on a
   256-bin histogram over [0, 1] (base R). The student can override it.
4. Form two candidate classes: pixels above the threshold ("light") and at or
   below it ("dark"). The student can restrict the search to one class.
5. Within each candidate class, apply a morphological opening with a square
   structuring element of side s pixels (student-set, default 3, 0 disables),
   then label 4-connected components.
6. Select the artifact: the component with the largest pixel area among those
   that do not touch the border of the crop, over all candidate classes. This
   replaces the border-majority polarity rule of revision 1, which failed on
   three of the four demo images because the scale bar and light margins occupy
   the border (audit finding F1). If every component touches the border, select
   the largest component overall and warn.
7. Fill holes: every 4-connected region outside the selected component that
   does not reach the border becomes part of it.
8. Pad the mask with one background pixel on every side and extract the
   iso-contour at level 0.5. Keep the longest contour. It is closed because the
   padded mask cannot touch the border.

Warnings shown to the student, never silent: no component found; every
component touched the border (step 6 fallback); the mask covers less than 1
percent or more than 90 percent of the crop; another candidate component has at
least one quarter of the selected area; the contour has fewer than 100 points
(the outline is then rejected and cannot be saved).

### 6.2 Orientation and landmarks

The contour is converted to a y-up system, consecutive duplicate vertices are
removed, and traversal is set to counterclockwise by the sign of the shoelace
area.

1. The student declares the tip direction d for the image (up, down, left,
   right). The setting carries over to the next image.
2. Compute the principal axes of the polygon from its area second moments. If
   the ratio of the larger to the smaller principal moment is below 1.1, set
   u = d and warn that the axis is poorly determined. Otherwise choose the
   principal axis with the larger absolute cosine to d. On an exact tie (both
   axes at 45 degrees) choose the major axis. Orient u so that u . d > 0.
3. Rotate so that u points up.
4. Tip T: the vertex with the largest y. If several vertices lie within 1e-9 of
   the maximum, choose the one nearest the midline of step 5; if still tied,
   the one with the smaller x.
5. Midline: the vertical line through the area centroid. An edge from vertex i
   to i+1 crosses the midline at x0 when x_i <= x0 < x_{i+1} or
   x_{i+1} <= x0 < x_i (half-open, so a vertex on the line is counted once).
   Base B: the crossing with the smallest y. B is inserted into the polygon as
   a new vertex by linear interpolation along its edge. If there are more than
   two crossings the app warns that the base may be misplaced.
6. Blunt-tip warning: if the cross-section width (section 6.4) at 5 percent of
   length below T exceeds 35 percent of the maximum width, the app warns that
   the tip end is blunt or broken and that T is a geometric choice. The outline
   can still be saved (author decision, 2026-11-17).
7. Manual override: the student clicks the tip, then the base. Each click is
   snapped to the nearest outline vertex. u runs from the base vertex to the
   tip vertex. If both clicks snap to the same vertex the app reports an error
   and keeps the previous landmarks. Manual landmarks are cleared when the crop
   changes.

T, B, and the resampled points do not depend on which vertex the contour
tracer started from, because every rule above refers to geometry only.

### 6.3 Corresponding points

T and B divide the polygon into two directed paths in counterclockwise order:
edge 1 from T to B (the left edge when the tip is up) and edge 2 from B to T.
Each path is sampled by linear interpolation at arc-length fractions j/100 of
its own length, j = 0..99, which includes the path's start and excludes its
end. This gives 200 distinct points: T at index 1, B at index 101.

Position from the tip, used for regions: index i in 1..100 has position
(i - 1)/100; index 101 has position 1; index i in 102..200 has position
(201 - i)/100. Mirror pairing: index i in 2..100 pairs with 202 - i; indices 1
and 101 pair with themselves.

Stated limitation (shown in the app): this correspondence is geometric. Equal
arc-length fractions are not guaranteed to fall on the same morphological
feature. A point with long barbs has more edge length in the shoulder zone than
a lanceolate point. Which face was photographed is arbitrary, so left and right
are not comparable across specimens. Region results pool both edges, which
gives each region the same definition on every specimen but does not remove
variation caused by the arbitrary face. Whether 100 points per edge and fixed
(non-sliding) points are adequate has not been tested and is stated as such.

### 6.4 Measurements

Computed from the oriented working-resolution contour (all contour vertices,
not the 200 resampled points):

- Length: extent along the axis (largest minus smallest y).
- Maximum width: extent at right angles to the axis (largest minus smallest x).
  Length and maximum width correspond to caliper measurements.
- Cross-section width at height y: the distance between the two outermost
  crossings of the horizontal line at y with the contour. It spans any notch.
- Position of the widest cross-section: cross-section width is evaluated at 200
# Image segmentation: grayscale image -> binary mask -> closed outline.
# Specification: docs/specs/2026-11-17_app_rewrite_spec.md, section 6.1.

# Otsu's threshold on a 256-bin histogram of values in [0, 1].
otsu_threshold <- function(values, bins = 256L) {
  values <- as.numeric(values)
  values <- values[is.finite(values)]
  if (length(values) == 0L) stop("No pixel values supplied.")
  rng <- range(values)
  if (rng[1] == rng[2]) return(rng[1])
  breaks <- seq(0, 1, length.out = bins + 1L)
  values <- pmin(pmax(values, 0), 1)
  counts <- tabulate(findInterval(values, breaks, rightmost.closed = TRUE), nbins = bins)
  mids <- (breaks[-1] + breaks[-(bins + 1L)]) / 2
  p <- counts / sum(counts)
  w0 <- cumsum(p)
  m0 <- cumsum(p * mids)
  mt <- m0[bins]
  w1 <- 1 - w0
  between <- ifelse(w0 > 0 & w1 > 0, (mt * w0 - m0)^2 / (w0 * w1), NA_real_)
  best <- which(between == max(between, na.rm = TRUE))
  # Upper edge of the last bin assigned to the lower class. When several bins
  # tie (an empty gap between two modes), take the middle of the gap.
  breaks[floor(stats::median(best)) + 1L]
}

# Values on the border of a matrix.
border_values <- function(m) {
  nx <- nrow(m); ny <- ncol(m)
  c(m[1, ], m[nx, ], m[-c(1, nx), 1], m[-c(1, nx), ny])
}

# Shift a logical matrix by k cells along dimension `dim`, filling with FALSE.
shift_matrix <- function(m, k, dim) {
  if (k == 0L) return(m)
  n <- if (dim == 1L) nrow(m) else ncol(m)
  out <- matrix(FALSE, nrow(m), ncol(m))
  if (abs(k) >= n) return(out)
  src <- if (k > 0L) seq_len(n - k) else seq.int(1L - k, n)
  dst <- src + k
  if (dim == 1L) out[dst, ] <- m[src, ] else out[, dst] <- m[, src]
  out
}

# Morphological opening with a square structuring element of side `side`
# pixels. Cells outside the matrix count as background. Side 0 or 1 does nothing.
open_square <- function(fg, side) {
  side <- as.integer(side)
  if (side <= 1L) return(fg)
  off <- seq.int(-((side - 1L) %/% 2L), side %/% 2L)
  pass <- function(m, offsets, op) {
    for (d in 1:2) m <- Reduce(op, lapply(offsets, function(k) shift_matrix(m, k, d)))
    m
  }
  pass(pass(fg, off, `&`), -off, `|`)
}

# 4-connected components of the TRUE cells of a logical matrix. Returns
# list(lab, table); table has one row per component: label, area, touches_border.
component_table <- function(fg) {
  lab <- as.matrix(imager::label(imager::as.cimg(fg * 1), high_connectivity = FALSE))
  ids <- sort(unique(lab[fg]))
  if (length(ids) == 0L) {
    return(list(lab = lab, table = data.frame(label = integer(0), area = integer(0),
                                              touches_border = logical(0))))
  }
  areas <- tabulate(match(lab[fg], ids), nbins = length(ids))
  border_ids <- unique(border_values(lab)[border_values(fg)])
  list(lab = lab, table = data.frame(label = ids, area = areas, touches_border = ids %in% border_ids))
}

# Segment the artifact from a grayscale matrix [x, y] with values in [0, 1].
# The artifact is the largest 4-connected region that does not touch the border
# of the matrix. With polarity "auto" both classes (lighter than the threshold,
# and at or below it) are searched. Specks are removed first by an opening with
# a square of side `speck_side` pixels. If every region touches the border, the
# largest region overall is used and a warning is returned. Holes in the
# selected region are filled.
# Returns list(mask, threshold, polarity, warnings); mask is NULL if nothing
# was found.
segment_image <- function(gray, threshold = NULL, polarity = c("auto", "light", "dark"),
                          speck_side = 3L) {
  polarity <- match.arg(polarity)
  g <- as.matrix(gray)
  if (is.null(threshold)) threshold <- otsu_threshold(g)
  above <- g > threshold
  classes <- switch(polarity, auto = c("light", "dark"), polarity)

  candidates <- lapply(classes, function(cl) {
    fg <- open_square(if (cl == "light") above else !above, speck_side)
    ct <- component_table(fg)
    list(class = cl, fg = fg, lab = ct$lab, table = ct$table)
  })

  best <- NULL
  for (interior_only in c(TRUE, FALSE)) {
    for (cand in candidates) {
      tab <- cand$table
      if (interior_only) tab <- tab[!tab$touches_border, , drop = FALSE]
      if (nrow(tab) == 0L) next
      i <- which.max(tab$area)
      if (is.null(best) || tab$area[i] > best$area) {
        best <- list(area = tab$area[i], label = tab$label[i], cand = cand, interior = interior_only,
                     n_other = sum(tab$area >= 0.25 * tab$area[i]) - 1L)
      }
    }
    if (!is.null(best)) break
  }
  if (is.null(best)) {
    return(list(mask = NULL, threshold = threshold, polarity = polarity,
                warnings = "No artifact was found. Adjust the threshold or the artifact brightness setting."))
  }

  mask <- best$cand$lab == best$label & best$cand$fg
  # Fill holes: regions outside the mask that do not connect to the border.
  outside <- component_table(!mask)
  open_ids <- outside$table$label[outside$table$touches_border]
  mask <- mask | (!mask & !(outside$lab %in% open_ids))

  warnings <- character(0)
  frac <- mean(mask)
  if (!best$interior)
    warnings <- c(warnings, "Every detected region touches the edge of the image or crop, so the artifact may be cut off or merged with the background. Redraw the crop so that background surrounds the artifact.")
  if (frac < 0.01)
    warnings <- c(warnings, "The detected artifact covers less than 1 percent of the image or crop. Crop closer to the artifact.")
  if (frac > 0.90)
    warnings <- c(warnings, "The detected region covers more than 90 percent of the image or crop. Check the artifact brightness setting.")
  if (best$n_other > 0)
    warnings <- c(warnings, sprintf("%d other object(s) at least one quarter the size of the selected one were detected and ignored. Crop to the artifact if the wrong object was selected.", best$n_other))
  list(mask = mask, threshold = threshold, polarity = best$cand$class, warnings = warnings)
}

# Closed boundary of a mask in pixel-index coordinates (x right, y down): the
# 0.5 iso-contour of the mask padded with one background pixel on every side,
# which guarantees closure. The longest contour is returned without a repeated
# end point.
mask_outline <- function(mask) {
  nx <- nrow(mask); ny <- ncol(mask)
  padded <- matrix(0, nx + 2L, ny + 2L)
  padded[2:(nx + 1L), 2:(ny + 1L)] <- mask * 1
  cs <- imager::contours(imager::as.cimg(padded), levels = 0.5)
  if (length(cs) == 0L) return(NULL)
  n <- vapply(cs, function(z) length(z$x), integer(1))
  z <- cs[[which.max(n)]]
  xy <- cbind(x = z$x - 1, y = z$y - 1)
  if (all(xy[1, ] == xy[nrow(xy), ])) xy <- xy[-nrow(xy), , drop = FALSE]
  xy
}
# Outline standardization: orientation, tip and base landmarks, corresponding
# points, and linear measurements. All functions take and return plain matrices.
# Specification: docs/specs/2026-11-17_app_rewrite_spec.md, sections 6.2 to 6.4.

N_EDGE <- 100L                 # points per edge
N_POINTS <- 2L * N_EDGE        # points per specimen
TIP_INDEX <- 1L
BASE_INDEX <- N_EDGE + 1L

direction_vector <- function(direction) {
  switch(direction,
         up = c(0, 1), down = c(0, -1), left = c(-1, 0), right = c(1, 0),
         stop("Unknown tip direction: ", direction))
}

# Signed area, centroid, and central second moments of a closed polygon.
polygon_moments <- function(xy) {
  x <- xy[, 1]; y <- xy[, 2]
  xn <- c(x[-1], x[1]); yn <- c(y[-1], y[1])
  cr <- x * yn - xn * y
  A <- sum(cr) / 2
  cx <- sum((x + xn) * cr) / (6 * A)
  cy <- sum((y + yn) * cr) / (6 * A)
  ixx <- sum((x^2 + x * xn + xn^2) * cr) / 12
  iyy <- sum((y^2 + y * yn + yn^2) * cr) / 12
  ixy <- sum((x * yn + 2 * x * y + 2 * xn * yn + xn * y) * cr) / 24
  cov <- matrix(c(ixx / A - cx^2, ixy / A - cx * cy,
                  ixy / A - cx * cy, iyy / A - cy^2), 2, 2)
  list(area = A, centroid = c(cx, cy), cov = cov)
}

# Convert an image-coordinate outline (y down) to y-up, counterclockwise, with
# consecutive duplicate points removed.
prepare_outline <- function(xy) {
  xy <- cbind(x = xy[, 1], y = -xy[, 2])
  n <- nrow(xy)
  if (n > 1L && all(abs(xy[1, ] - xy[n, ]) < 1e-12)) xy <- xy[-n, , drop = FALSE]
  prev <- xy[c(nrow(xy), seq_len(nrow(xy) - 1L)), , drop = FALSE]
  xy <- xy[sqrt(rowSums((xy - prev)^2)) > 1e-12, , drop = FALSE]
  if (nrow(xy) < 3L) stop("The outline has fewer than three distinct points.")
  if (polygon_moments(xy)$area < 0) xy <- xy[nrow(xy):1, , drop = FALSE]
  xy
}

# Unit vector along the tip-base axis, pointing toward the tip: the principal
# axis of the polygon's area with the larger absolute cosine to the declared
# direction (the major axis on an exact tie). When the principal moments differ
# by less than `min_ratio` the declared direction itself is returned.
# Returns list(u, isotropic).
axis_direction <- function(xy, direction, min_ratio = 1.1) {
  d <- direction_vector(direction)
  e <- eigen(polygon_moments(xy)$cov, symmetric = TRUE)
  if (e$values[1] / e$values[2] < min_ratio) return(list(u = d, isotropic = TRUE))
  dots <- as.numeric(crossprod(e$vectors, d))
  # eigen() lists the major axis first and which.max takes the first maximum,
  # so an exact 45 degree tie resolves to the major axis.
  k <- which.max(round(abs(dots), 9))
  u <- e$vectors[, k] * sign(dots[k])
  list(u = u / sqrt(sum(u^2)), isotropic = FALSE)
}

# Rotation matrix R such that R %*% u = (0, 1).
axis_rotation <- function(u) matrix(c(u[2], u[1], -u[1], u[2]), 2, 2)

# Rotate points so that unit vector u maps to (0, 1).
rotate_axis_up <- function(xy, u) {
  out <- xy %*% t(axis_rotation(u))
  colnames(out) <- c("x", "y")
  out
}

# Crossings of the vertical line x = x0 with a closed polygon. An edge from
# vertex i to i+1 crosses when x_i <= x0 < x_{i+1} or x_{i+1} <= x0 < x_i, so a
# vertex on the line is counted once.
# Returns a data frame: edge (index of the edge's first vertex), y.
vertical_crossings <- function(xy, x0) {
  n <- nrow(xy)
  x <- xy[, 1]; y <- xy[, 2]
  j <- c(2:n, 1L)
  i <- which((x <= x0 & x[j] > x0) | (x > x0 & x[j] <= x0))
  t <- (x0 - x[i]) / (x[j[i]] - x[i])
  data.frame(edge = i, y = y[i] + t * (y[j[i]] - y[i]))
}

# Points at given arc-length fractions along an open polyline.
resample_path <- function(path, fractions) {
  s <- c(0, cumsum(sqrt(rowSums(diff(path)^2))))
  target <- fractions * s[length(s)]
  cbind(x = stats::approx(s, path[, 1], target, ties = "ordered")$y,
        y = stats::approx(s, path[, 2], target, ties = "ordered")$y)
}

# Position of each of the N_POINTS points from tip (0) to base (1).
point_positions <- function() {
  c((0:(N_EDGE - 1L)) / N_EDGE, 1, ((N_EDGE - 1L):1) / N_EDGE)
}

# Index of the point at the same tip-to-base position on the opposite edge.
mirror_index <- function() {
  out <- N_POINTS + 2L - seq_len(N_POINTS)
  out[TIP_INDEX] <- TIP_INDEX
  out
}

# Corresponding points from an oriented outline whose first row is the tip and
# whose row `base_row` is the base: each directed edge (tip to base, base to
# tip) sampled at arc-length fractions j / N_EDGE, j = 0 .. N_EDGE - 1.
points_from_outline <- function(outline, base_row) {
  n <- nrow(outline)
  if (base_row < 2L || base_row > n) stop("The base row is outside the outline.")
  left <- outline[1:base_row, , drop = FALSE]
  right <- rbind(outline[base_row:n, , drop = FALSE], outline[1, , drop = FALSE])
  fr <- (0:(N_EDGE - 1L)) / N_EDGE
  pts <- rbind(resample_path(left, fr), resample_path(right, fr))
  rownames(pts) <- NULL
  pts
}

# Cross-section width of an oriented outline at height y: the distance between
# the outermost crossings of the horizontal line, spanning any notch.
cross_section_width <- function(outline, y) {
  cr <- vertical_crossings(outline[, 2:1, drop = FALSE], y)
  if (nrow(cr) < 2L) 0 else diff(range(cr$y))
}

# TRUE when the cross-section 5 percent of the length below the tip is wider
# than 35 percent of the maximum width.
blunt_tip <- function(outline, depth = 0.05, share = 0.35) {
  yr <- range(outline[, 2])
  cross_section_width(outline, yr[2] - depth * diff(yr)) > share * diff(range(outline[, 1]))
}

# Standardize a prepared outline.
#   xy:        prepared outline (y up, counterclockwise)
#   direction: declared tip direction ("up", "down", "left", "right")
#   tip, base: optional manual landmarks as c(x, y) in the same coordinates;
#              each is snapped to the nearest outline vertex
# Returns list(points (N_POINTS x 2, tip first), outline (oriented contour, tip
# up, tip first, base at row base_row), base_row, tip, base, tip_input and
# base_input (landmarks in the input coordinates), axis, warnings).
standardize_outline <- function(xy, direction = "up", tip = NULL, base = NULL) {
  warnings <- character(0)
  manual <- !is.null(tip) && !is.null(base)
  n <- nrow(xy)

  if (manual) {
    nearest <- function(p) which.min((xy[, 1] - p[1])^2 + (xy[, 2] - p[2])^2)
    ti <- nearest(as.numeric(tip)); bi <- nearest(as.numeric(base))
    if (ti == bi) stop("The tip and base landmarks fall on the same outline point. Click two different places.")
    u <- xy[ti, ] - xy[bi, ]
    u <- u / sqrt(sum(u^2))
    rot <- rotate_axis_up(xy, u)
    rot <- rot[c(ti:n, seq_len(ti - 1L)), , drop = FALSE]
    bi <- ((bi - ti) %% n) + 1L
    left <- rot[1:bi, , drop = FALSE]
    right <- rbind(rot[bi:n, , drop = FALSE], rot[1, , drop = FALSE])
  } else {
    ax <- axis_direction(xy, direction)
    u <- ax$u
    if (ax$isotropic)
      warnings <- c(warnings, "This outline is nearly as wide as it is long, so its long axis could not be determined. The declared tip direction was used as the axis. Check the tip and base markers, and set them by hand if they are misplaced.")
    rot <- rotate_axis_up(xy, u)
    cx <- polygon_moments(rot)$centroid[1]
    cand <- which(rot[, 2] >= max(rot[, 2]) - 1e-9)
    if (length(cand) > 1L) {
      # Several vertices share the maximum: nearest the midline, then smaller x.
      off <- round(abs(rot[cand, 1] - cx), 9)
      cand <- cand[off == min(off)]
      cand <- cand[which.min(rot[cand, 1])]
    }
    rot <- rot[c(cand:n, seq_len(cand - 1L)), , drop = FALSE]
    cr <- vertical_crossings(rot, cx)
    if (nrow(cr) < 2L) stop("The midline does not cross the outline.")
    k <- which.min(cr$y)
    e <- cr$edge[k]
    bpt <- c(x = cx, y = cr$y[k])
    if (nrow(cr) > 2L)
      warnings <- c(warnings, "The midline crosses the outline more than twice (for example through a basal notch or a barb). The base marker was placed at the crossing farthest from the tip. Check it, and set the tip and base by hand if it is misplaced.")
    # The half-open crossing rule means the base point can coincide with the
    # edge's first vertex but never with its second.
    on_vertex <- sqrt(sum((rot[e, ] - bpt)^2)) < 1e-12
    left <- if (on_vertex) rot[1:e, , drop = FALSE] else rbind(rot[1:e, , drop = FALSE], bpt)
    rest <- if (e < n) rot[(e + 1L):n, , drop = FALSE] else rot[0, , drop = FALSE]
    right <- rbind(bpt, rest, rot[1, , drop = FALSE])
  }

  outline <- rbind(left, right[-c(1L, nrow(right)), , drop = FALSE])
  rownames(outline) <- NULL
  base_row <- nrow(left)
  if (!manual && blunt_tip(outline))
    warnings <- c(warnings, "The tip end of this outline is blunt or broken. The tip marker is at the point farthest along the axis, which may not be where the original tip was. You can set the tip and base by hand.")

  pts <- points_from_outline(outline, base_row)
  back <- function(p) as.numeric(matrix(p, 1) %*% axis_rotation(u))
  list(points = pts, outline = outline, base_row = base_row,
       tip = pts[TIP_INDEX, ], base = pts[BASE_INDEX, ],
       tip_input = back(pts[TIP_INDEX, ]), base_input = back(pts[BASE_INDEX, ]),
       axis = u, warnings = warnings)
}

# Cross-section width at `n_levels` heights: the midpoints of equal slices of
# the length. `height` is the fraction of length above the lowest point.
width_profile <- function(outline, n_levels = 200L) {
  yr <- range(outline[, 2])
  levels <- yr[1] + (seq_len(n_levels) - 0.5) / n_levels * diff(yr)
  data.frame(height = (levels - yr[1]) / diff(yr),
             width = vapply(levels, function(l) cross_section_width(outline, l), numeric(1)))
}

# Linear measurements of an oriented outline (tip up), in the outline's pixel
# units and in millimetres when px_per_mm is given.
#   length: extent along the axis; width: extent at right angles to it;
#   widest_position: height of the widest cross-section as a fraction of length
#   above the lowest point (ties go to the level nearest the base).
measure_outline <- function(outline, px_per_mm = NA_real_) {
  len <- diff(range(outline[, 2]))
  wid <- diff(range(outline[, 1]))
  prof <- width_profile(outline)
  area <- abs(polygon_moments(outline)$area)
  s <- if (length(px_per_mm) == 1L && is.finite(px_per_mm) && px_per_mm > 0) px_per_mm else NA_real_
  data.frame(
    length_px = len, width_px = wid, area_px2 = area,
    length_mm = len / s, width_mm = wid / s, area_mm2 = area / s^2,
    length_width_ratio = len / wid,
    widest_position = prof$height[which.max(round(prof$width, 9))]
  )
}

